                                ;============================================================
                                ; SEARCH  (/ ? n N)
                                ;
                                ; vi's search on WordMaster's matcher.  What it is NOT: vim searches with a
                                ; REGULAR EXPRESSION and this searches for the literal string typed.  An 8080
                                ; has no room for a regex engine; COMMANDS.md records the deviation.  (WM's own
                                ; search wildcards -- ^A any, ^S separator, ^O not -- still reach WCMATCH from
                                ; here, because MATCHF/MATCHB compare through it, but they are WordMaster's
                                ; syntax, not vim's, and nothing advertises them.)
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SRCH, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - '/' starts one character PAST the cursor and lands ON the match's first
                                ;     character; '?' finds the last match that ends before the cursor.
                                ;   - 'wrapscan' is on: a search that runs off one end of the file starts
                                ;     again at the other, so '/' on the file's ONLY match finds it again.
                                ;   - 'n' repeats the last search's DIRECTION and 'N' reverses it, so 'n'
                                ;     after a '?' keeps going backward.
                                ;   - An empty pattern ('/' then <CR>) repeats the last pattern.
                                ;   - A count repeats the search: '2/a' is the second match, '15n' fifteen
                                ;     n's.  Not found leaves the cursor exactly where it was, at any count.
                                ;   - The window is placed as 'G' places it -- least scroll when the match is
                                ;     near, centred when it is far -- which is why this ends in GPLACE.
                                ;
                                ; This replaces a byte-at-a-time search that ran through CHARAT over logical
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-112


                                ; offsets: it could only see the resident window (so a match further into a
                                ; paged file did not exist), had no '?' or 'N', and failed silently.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; SUBSTITUTE  (:s  :%s  :N,Ms  and the 'g' flag)
                                ;
                                ; vi's substitute on WordMaster's matcher, so it finds the same LITERAL string
                                ; the search does -- not a regular expression.  COMMANDS.md records that
                                ; deviation for '/' and it is the same one here, which is why nothing below
                                ; honours '&', '\1' or '~'.  The pattern goes into the SEARCH's own buffer
                                ; because vim's ':s' also sets the last search pattern, so 'n' afterwards
                                ; repeats it.
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SUBST, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - ':s' changes the FIRST match on the cursor's line, ':s...g' every match,
                                ;     and the scan goes on PAST the replacement, so a replacement that
                                ;     contains the pattern is not substituted again.
                                ;   - the cursor lands on the first non-blank of the LAST line changed, placed
                                ;     exactly as 'G' places a line -- which is what GZONE before and GPLACE
                                ;     after are for, and it is measured: ':12800,12800s' leaves the last line
                                ;     on row 22 (top 012778) and ':100,105s' leaves line 105 on row 11.
                                ;   - a pattern that is nowhere in the range changes nothing and moves nothing.
                                ;
                                ; A whole substitute is ONE change for 'u': the second UNDBEG, at the first
                                ; match, is what makes the undo's cursor the first match (where vim leaves it)
                                ; rather than the line the ':' was typed on.  A substitute of more than about a
                                ; dozen lines overruns the undo region, which is accepted -- but it SAYS so,
                                ; both when it happens and when 'u' is then pressed.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;EXADDR -- the optional line range before an ex command: '%' (the whole file),
                                ;'N', or 'N,M'.  SRNGF = 0 when there is none, and SRNG2 = 0FFFFH is the last
                                ;line, whatever it turns out to be.  HL comes back on the command letter.
                                ;
                                ; It does NOT consume the blanks after itself: VC_EX's SKIPB does that, so
                                ; ':2,4 s/a/Z/' reads as ':2,4s/a/Z/' the way ex has always taken it.  The one
                                ; place a blank is still refused is INSIDE the range, around the comma
                                ; (':2, 4s'), which ex accepts and this does not -- the comma is read here off
                                ; DE mid-number and skipping there costs more than the position is worth.
  2474'                         EXADDR:
  2474'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2475'   32 2ACE'              	STA	SRNGF
  2478'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2479'   FE 25                 	CPI	'%'
  247B'   CA 24A4'              	JZ	EXA_AL
  247E'   EB                    	XCHG			; DE walks the line, HL takes the number
  247F'   CD 24B9'              	CALL	EXNUM
  2482'   DA 24A2'              	JC	EXA_NO
  2485'   22 2ACF'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  2488'   22 2AD1'              	SHLD	SRNG2		; 'N' alone is the single line 'N,N'
  248B'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  248C'   FE 2C                 	CPI	','
  248E'   C2 249B'              	JNZ	EXA_YS
  2491'   13                    	INX	D
  2492'   CD 24B9'              	CALL	EXNUM
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-113


  2495'   DA 24A2'              	JC	EXA_NO		; ',' with no number: not a range at all
  2498'   22 2AD1'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  249B'                         EXA_YS:
  249B'   EB                    	XCHG
  249C'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  249E'   32 2ACE'              	STA	SRNGF
  24A1'   C9                    	RET
  24A2'                         EXA_NO:
  24A2'   EB                    	XCHG
  24A3'   C9                    	RET
  24A4'                         EXA_AL:
  24A4'   23                    	INX	H
  24A5'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  24A6'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  24A9'   22 2ACF'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  24AC'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  24AF'   22 2AD1'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  24B2'   E1                    	POP	H
  24B3'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  24B5'   32 2ACE'              	STA	SRNGF
  24B8'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EXNUM -- a decimal number at DE -> HL, DE left past it.  CY when there is no
                                ;digit there at all.
  24B9'                         EXNUM:
  24B9'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  24BA'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  24BC'   D8                    	RC
  24BD'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  24BF'   3F                    	CMC
  24C0'   D8                    	RC
  24C1'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  24C4'                         EXN_LP:
  24C4'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  24C5'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  24C7'   DA 24E1'              	JC	EXN_DN
  24CA'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  24CC'   D2 24E1'              	JNC	EXN_DN
  24CF'   13                    	INX	D
  24D0'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  24D1'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  24D2'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; BC = the digit
  24D4'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  24D5'   54                    	MOV	D,H
  24D6'   5D                    	MOV	E,L
  24D7'   29                    	DAD	H		; *2
  24D8'   29                    	DAD	H		; *4
  24D9'   19                    	DAD	D		; *5
  24DA'   29                    	DAD	H		; *10
  24DB'   D1                    	POP	D
  24DC'   09                    	DAD	B
  24DD'   C1                    	POP	B
  24DE'   C3 24C4'              	JMP	EXN_LP
  24E1'                         EXN_DN:
  24E1'   B7                    	ORA	A		; CY clear: there was a number
  24E2'   C9                    	RET
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-114


                                
                                ;EX_S -- ':s': the delimiter, the pattern, the replacement, an optional 'g'.
  24E3'                         EX_S:
  24E3'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  24E4'   B7                    	ORA	A
  24E5'   CA 2527'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s' alone -- repeating the last one is not
                                				;   built (COMMANDS.md)
  24E8'   32 2AD3'              	STA	SDELIM
  24EB'   23                    	INX	H
  24EC'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  24EF'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; the pattern's capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  24F2'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  24F3'   CD 252D'              	CALL	SCOPY
  24F6'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  24F7'   B7                    	ORA	A
  24F8'   CA 2527'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s//x/' -- an empty pattern matches nothing
  24FB'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		; WM's RDSTR layout: the length below the string
  24FE'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2500'   32 2A9B'              	STA	SHAVE		; 'n' repeats it, as after a '/'
  2503'   11 2AE5'              	LXI	D,SREPL
  2506'   0E 24                 	MVI	C,SREPMX
  2508'   CD 252D'              	CALL	SCOPY
  250B'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  250C'   32 2AE4'              	STA	SREPLN
  250F'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2510'   32 2AD4'              	STA	SGFLAG
  2513'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2514'   FE 67                 	CPI	'g'
  2516'   C2 251F'              	JNZ	EX_SG
  2519'   23                    	INX	H
  251A'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  251C'   32 2AD4'              	STA	SGFLAG
  251F'                         EX_SG:
  251F'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2520'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2521'   C2 22BE'              	JNZ	EX_TRL		; 'Trailing chars: ' -- vim's, shortened
  2524'   C3 254D'              	JMP	SUBGO
  2527'                         SUBNOP:
  2527'   21 2376'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  252A'   C3 22CC'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;SCOPY -- the text at HL up to SDELIM (or the line's end) into [DE], C its
                                ;capacity: B = how many were taken, HL past the delimiter, and [DE] is left
                                ;0-terminated as WM's RDSTR leaves a string.
  252D'                         SCOPY:
  252D'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  252F'                         SCP_LP:
  252F'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2530'   B9                    	CMP	C
  2531'   D2 254A'              	JNC	SCP_DN		; full: what is left stays on the line, and so
                                				;   comes back as trailing characters
  2534'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2535'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2536'   CA 254A'              	JZ	SCP_DN		; the line ended: no closing delimiter wanted
  2539'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  253A'   21 2AD3'              	LXI	H,SDELIM
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-115


  253D'   BE                    	CMP	M
  253E'   E1                    	POP	H
  253F'   CA 2549'              	JZ	SCP_SK
  2542'   12                    	STAX	D
  2543'   13                    	INX	D
  2544'   23                    	INX	H
  2545'   04                    	INR	B
  2546'   C3 252F'              	JMP	SCP_LP
  2549'                         SCP_SK:
  2549'   23                    	INX	H
  254A'                         SCP_DN:
  254A'   AF                    	XRA	A
  254B'   12                    	STAX	D
  254C'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBGO -- run the substitute.  GZONE comes first, before anything moves: the
                                ;landing is placed as 'G' places a line, and GPLACE measures it against the
                                ;screen's top line as it was when the command was typed.
  254D'                         SUBGO:
  254D'   CD 0F75'              	CALL	GZONE
  2550'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	LNDLT
  2553'   22 2AE2'              	SHLD	SLDLTS		; the walk moves the cursor; a substitute that
  2556'   21 2ADF'              	LXI	H,SOFFS		;   finds nothing puts both back, as the
  2559'   CD 044F'              	CALL	FPSTO		;   search's SN_BAD does
  255C'   AF                    	XRA	A
  255D'   32 2AD5'              	STA	SANY
  2560'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  2563'   22 2AD7'              	SHLD	SBACK
  2566'   3A 2ACE'              	LDA	SRNGF
  2569'   B7                    	ORA	A
  256A'   CA 257C'              	JZ	SUB_1L
                                	;--- ':N,Ms' / ':%s': to the range's first line, then walk it ---
  256D'   CD 1E48'              	CALL	EXRNG		; SLINES = the lines in the range
  2570'   DA 25F8'              	JC	SUBBAD
  2573'   2A 2ACF'              	LHLD	SRNG1
  2576'   CD 0EE3'              	CALL	GOLN
  2579'   C3 2582'              	JMP	SUBLP
  257C'                         SUB_1L:
  257C'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  257F'   22 2AD9'              	SHLD	SLINES
  2582'                         SUBLP:
  2582'   CD 25FE'              	CALL	SUBLIN		; A = 1 when this line changed
  2585'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2586'   CA 2594'              	JZ	SUB_NX
  2589'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  258B'   32 2AD5'              	STA	SANY
  258E'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  2591'   22 2AD7'              	SHLD	SBACK		; the cursor is ON the last line changed
  2594'                         SUB_NX:
  2594'   2A 2AD9'              	LHLD	SLINES
  2597'   2B                    	DCX	H
  2598'   22 2AD9'              	SHLD	SLINES
  259B'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  259C'   B5                    	ORA	L
  259D'   CA 25BB'              	JZ	SUBDON		; the range is spent
  25A0'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-116


  25A3'   AF                    	XRA	A
  25A4'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; the next line, paging with it
  25A7'   3A 2AD5'              	LDA	SANY
  25AA'   B7                    	ORA	A
  25AB'   CA 25B5'              	JZ	SUB_AE		; nothing changed yet: nothing to count from
  25AE'   2A 2AD7'              	LHLD	SBACK
  25B1'   23                    	INX	H
  25B2'   22 2AD7'              	SHLD	SBACK		; every step taken after the last change has
                                				;   to be walked back at the end -- including
                                				;   this one, when it lands past the last line
  25B5'                         SUB_AE:
  25B5'   CD 0A1D'              	CALL	ATEOF
  25B8'   D2 2582'              	JNC	SUBLP		; ... unless that ran off the end of the file
  25BB'                         SUBDON:
  25BB'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the walk left the count and direction set
  25BE'   3A 2AD5'              	LDA	SANY
  25C1'   B7                    	ORA	A
  25C2'   CA 25E0'              	JZ	SUBNFD
  25C5'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  25C7'   32 2A1C'              	STA	MODF		; the text changed, so ':w' has work to do.  NOT
                                				;   MARKMOD: that also sets DOTCHG, and '.' does
                                				;   not repeat a substitute in vim either (that
                                				;   is '&', which is not built)
  25CA'   2A 2AD7'              	LHLD	SBACK
  25CD'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  25CE'   B5                    	ORA	L
  25CF'   CA 25DA'              	JZ	SUB_PL
  25D2'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH
  25D4'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; back to the last line changed
  25D7'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT
  25DA'                         SUB_PL:
  25DA'   CD 0F8D'              	CALL	GPLACE		; the landing: first non-blank, placed as 'G'
  25DD'   C3 077B'              	JMP	SETFUL		; GPLACE asks for R_MOVE when the target is
                                				;   which rewrote the line under the cursor.
                                				; And that repaint is why a substitute too big
                                				;   for the undo does NOT say so here: EXMSG
                                				;   draws the bottom row there and then, and
                                				;   the full paint this needs takes the status
                                				;   row back (SCRN.MAC says so itself).  'u'
                                				;   carries the message instead -- UNDWHY = 2,
                                				;   at the moment the reader asks for the undo
                                				;   and can still act on the answer.
  25E0'                         SUBNFD:
  25E0'   21 2ADF'              	LXI	H,SOFFS
  25E3'   CD 03F6'              	CALL	BACKTO
  25E6'   2A 2AE2'              	LHLD	SLDLTS
  25E9'   22 0000*              	SHLD	LNDLT		; nothing changed: the row the cursor was on
  25EC'   21 2356'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  25EF'   CD 22CC'              	CALL	EXERR
  25F2'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  25F5'   C3 22C7'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
  25F8'                         SUBBAD:
  25F8'   21 2344'              	LXI	H,SNOT
  25FB'   C3 22C1'              	JMP	EXERT
                                
                                ;SUBLIN -- the substitute on the cursor's line: the first match, or every match
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-117


                                ;when 'g' was given.  A = 1 when the line changed.  The cursor is left at the
                                ;line's start either way, so the walk to the next line always starts from one.
                                ;Every offset is re-derived from the cursor after each change, never held
                                ;across one: an insert can page (':%s/0/ZZ/' grows a 100 K file by 12800
                                ;bytes) and paging shifts every logical offset in the window.
  25FE'                         SUBLIN:
  25FE'   AF                    	XRA	A
  25FF'   32 2AD6'              	STA	SLCHG
  2602'   CD 267D'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  2605'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET0		; the line has to be RESIDENT before CEND can
  2608'                         SUBL_R:				;   find its end: the pager keeps the CURSOR in
  2608'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET		;   the window, not the whole line it sits on,
  260B'   DA 2613'              	JC	SUBL_L		;   and EOLNL only ever scans resident text --
  260E'   FE 0A                 	CPI	LF		;   so a line straddling the resident end came
  2610'   C2 2608'              	JNZ	SUBL_R		;   out short and went unsubstituted (line 179
                                				;   of the 66 K indented file, which starts at
                                				;   byte 2047).  RGET pages through NEEDIN, as
                                				;   every other line reader here does.
  2613'                         SUBL_L:
                                	;--- the scan window: the cursor to the line's content end ---
  2613'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  2616'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2617'   CD 1501'              	CALL	CEND
  261A'   D1                    	POP	D
  261B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = the bytes left on the line
  261E'   23                    	INX	H		; FWDCNT's own arithmetic, inline: the span it
  261F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		;   would measure, plus one, less the pattern.
  2622'   7C                    	MOV	A,H		;   (FWDCNT needs a real pointer pair and this
  2623'   B7                    	ORA	A		;   span is a line, not a pair.)
  2624'   FA 2676'              	JM	SUBL_D		; what is left is shorter than the pattern
  2627'   44                    	MOV	B,H
  2628'   4D                    	MOV	C,L		; BC = the start positions a match can take
  2629'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  262C'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; with the gap at the cursor, the line runs
  262F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF		;   forward from GAPEND
  2632'   DA 2676'              	JC	SUBL_D
  2635'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends past the match; back to its start
  2638'   EB                    	XCHG
  2639'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND
  263C'   EB                    	XCHG
  263D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = how far into the scan the match is
  2640'   EB                    	XCHG
  2641'   D5                    	PUSH	D		; CUROFF does NOT keep DE (it reads TXTBEG into
  2642'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF		;   it), so the delta goes on the stack
  2645'   D1                    	POP	D
  2646'   19                    	DAD	D		; ... and so the match's logical offset
  2647'   CD 0000*              	CALL	GOTO
                                	;--- the first change of the whole substitute re-arms the undo, so 'u'
                                	;    lands on the first match, which is where vim leaves it ---
  264A'   3A 2AD5'              	LDA	SANY
  264D'   B7                    	ORA	A
  264E'   C2 265B'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  2651'   3A 2AD6'              	LDA	SLCHG
  2654'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2655'   C2 265B'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  2658'   CD 0000*              	CALL	UNDHER		; the cursor snapshot only: UNDBEG would re-run
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-118


                                				;   UNDGRW mid-command, and growing the region
                                				;   where a primitive can see it corrupts the
                                				;   gap ('COPY ERR')
  265B'                         SUBL_C:
  265B'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCHBF
  265E'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  265F'   26 00                 	MVI	H,0
  2661'   CD 0000*              	CALL	DELFWD		; the match goes
  2664'   11 2AE4'              	LXI	D,SREPLN	; ... and the replacement takes its place, the
  2667'   CD 0000*              	CALL	INSSTR		;   way WM splices one: the length byte with
                                				;   its string above it, 'u' capture included
  266A'                         SUBL_I:
  266A'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  266C'   32 2AD6'              	STA	SLCHG
  266F'   3A 2AD4'              	LDA	SGFLAG
  2672'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2673'   C2 2613'              	JNZ	SUBL_L		; 'g': on from PAST the replacement
  2676'                         SUBL_D:
  2676'   CD 267D'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  2679'   3A 2AD6'              	LDA	SLCHG
  267C'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBBOL -- the cursor to the start of the line it is on.
  267D'                         SUBBOL:
  267D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  2680'   CD 05E4'              	CALL	BOL
  2683'   C3 0000*              	JMP	GOTO
                                
                                ;VC_SRCH -- '/' and '?': read the pattern, then search.  C is the key, which
                                ; is both the prompt character and the direction.
  2686'                         VC_SRCH:
  2686'   CD 075A'              	CALL	SETMOV
  2689'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  268A'   CD 068B'              	CALL	GETCNT
  268D'   22 2AC4'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2690'   C1                    	POP	B
  2691'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2692'   FE 2F                 	CPI	'/'
  2694'   3E 00                 	MVI	A,0
  2696'   CA 269B'              	JZ	VS_DIR
  2699'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH		; '?' -- backward
  269B'                         VS_DIR:
  269B'   32 2A99'              	STA	SDIR
  269E'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST	; the line is read straight into the pattern
  26A1'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; its capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  26A4'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  26A5'   CD 1BE4'              	CALL	RDLINE
  26A8'   B7                    	ORA	A
  26A9'   C0                    	RNZ			; cancelled: nothing happens at all
  26AA'   3A 2A71'              	LDA	RLLEN
  26AD'   B7                    	ORA	A
  26AE'   CA 26C3'              	JZ	VS_OLD		; an empty line repeats the last pattern --
                                				;   RDLINE stored nothing, so it is still there
  26B1'   5F                    	MOV	E,A
  26B2'   16 00                 	MVI	D,0
  26B4'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-119


  26B7'   19                    	DAD	D
  26B8'   36 00                 	MVI	M,0		; 0-terminated, and the length below it:
  26BA'   7B                    	MOV	A,E
  26BB'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		;   WM's RDSTR layout, built by hand
  26BE'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  26C0'   32 2A9B'              	STA	SHAVE
  26C3'                         VS_OLD:
  26C3'   3A 2A9B'              	LDA	SHAVE
  26C6'   B7                    	ORA	A
  26C7'   CA 26D3'              	JZ	VS_NOP		; '/' <CR> with nothing ever searched for
  26CA'   3A 2A99'              	LDA	SDIR
  26CD'   32 2A9A'              	STA	SDIRE
  26D0'   C3 26FC'              	JMP	SRCHN
  26D3'                         VS_NOP:
  26D3'   21 2376'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  26D6'   C3 22CC'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;VC_N / VCB_N -- 'n' repeats the last search, 'N' reverses its direction.
  26D9'                         VC_N:
  26D9'   CD 075A'              	CALL	SETMOV
  26DC'   3A 2A99'              	LDA	SDIR
  26DF'   C3 26E9'              	JMP	VN_GO
  26E2'                         VCB_N:
  26E2'   CD 075A'              	CALL	SETMOV
  26E5'   3A 2A99'              	LDA	SDIR
  26E8'   2F                    	CMA			; the other way: 0 <-> FFH
  26E9'                         VN_GO:
  26E9'   32 2A9A'              	STA	SDIRE
  26EC'   CD 068B'              	CALL	GETCNT
  26EF'   22 2AC4'              	SHLD	SCNT
  26F2'   3A 2A9B'              	LDA	SHAVE
  26F5'   B7                    	ORA	A
  26F6'   CA 26D3'              	JZ	VS_NOP
  26F9'   C3 26FC'              	JMP	SRCHN
                                
                                ;SRCHN -- the count, one whole-file search each.  Not found at ANY repeat
                                ; leaves the cursor where the command started, as vim does, and says so; the
                                ; message wants the screen left alone, which is what EXERR's R_MOVE is for.
  26FC'                         SRCHN:
  26FC'   CD 0F75'              	CALL	GZONE		; the screen's top line, before the cursor moves
  26FF'   CD 06B4'              	CALL	BRKON		; where it started (the sweep moves the cursor
                                				;   with PUTCUR, which does not maintain LNDLT;
                                				;   GOTO does), and a ^C may stop it
  2702'                         SN_LP:
  2702'   CD 2737'              	CALL	SRCHGO
  2705'   DA 2722'              	JC	SN_BAD
  2708'   2A 2AC4'              	LHLD	SCNT
  270B'   2B                    	DCX	H
  270C'   22 2AC4'              	SHLD	SCNT
  270F'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2710'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2711'   C2 2702'              	JNZ	SN_LP
  2714'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the sweep left CMDCNT wide open (and DIRFLG
                                				;   backward): WM clears both per command
  2717'   CD 06EB'              	CALL	BRKOFF
  271A'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-120


  271C'   32 2AA6'              	STA	GPKEEP		; land on the match, not the first non-blank
  271F'   C3 0F8D'              	JMP	GPLACE		; (which sets LNDLT itself)
  2722'                         SN_BAD:
  2722'   CD 06EB'              	CALL	BRKOFF
  2725'   DA 0703'              	JC	BRKBAK		; stopped, which is not the same as not found
  2728'   CD 06F6'              	CALL	RSTPL		; nothing moved: the row the cursor was on
  272B'   21 2356'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  272E'   CD 22CC'              	CALL	EXERR
  2731'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  2734'   C3 22C7'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
                                
                                ;SRCHGO -- one search of the whole file, wrapping once: from the cursor to the
                                ; end in SDIRE's direction, then from the other end back to it.  CY = the
                                ; pattern is nowhere in the file (and the cursor is left at an end of it --
                                ; SRCHN is what puts it back).
  2737'                         SRCHGO:
  2737'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2739'   32 2ACD'              	STA	SSKIP		; this pass starts one past the cursor
  273C'   CD 2752'              	CALL	SRCHW
  273F'   D0                    	RNC
  2740'   CD 06EF'              	CALL	BRKQ
  2743'   D8                    	RC			; stopped: no second pass
  2744'   3A 2A9A'              	LDA	SDIRE
  2747'   2F                    	CMA
  2748'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLMAX		; to the end the wrapped pass starts from
  274B'   AF                    	XRA	A
  274C'   32 2ACD'              	STA	SSKIP		; ... but the wrapped pass starts AT it, or a
                                				;   match on the file's very first character
                                				;   would be stepped over and never found
  274F'   C3 2752'              	JMP	SRCHW
                                
                                ;SRCHW -- WM FINDA (WM 1943): the file from the cursor on, one resident
                                ; window at a time.  A miss moves the cursor to that window's edge and pages
                                ; one step (SCRLST), so the search runs off the window and through the file;
                                ; CY when the file runs out.  PAGESTP only pages while the count is unspent,
                                ; so the sweep opens CMDCNT wide, as MVLMAX does.  WM's FINDA then puts the
                                ; cursor exactly at the file's end (WM 1949); nothing here needs that -- the
                                ; caller either wraps to the other end or restores the cursor outright.
  2752'                         SRCHW:
  2752'   3A 2A9A'              	LDA	SDIRE
  2755'   32 0000*              	STA	DIRFLG
  2758'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  275B'   22 2A19'              	SHLD	CMDCNT
  275E'   AF                    	XRA	A
  275F'   32 0000*              	STA	AUXCNT
  2762'                         SW_LP:
  2762'   CD 06EF'              	CALL	BRKQ		; a ^C spent the count, here or on the way to
  2765'   D8                    	RC			;   the wrapped pass: SCRLST no longer pages
  2766'   CD 2772'              	CALL	SRCH1
  2769'   D0                    	RNC
  276A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SCRLST
  276D'   D2 2762'              	JNC	SW_LP
  2770'   37                    	STC
  2771'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SRCH1 -- WM FINDB (WM 1984) without its RDSTR: one search inside the
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-121


                                ; resident window, DIRFLG picking the direction.  CY = not in this window; on
                                ; a hit the cursor is ON the match's first character.
  2772'                         SRCH1:
  2772'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  2775'   3A 0000*              	LDA	DIRFLG
  2778'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2779'   FA 279D'              	JM	SR1_BK
  277C'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,GAPEND
  277F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  2782'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; WM loads the pointer BEFORE testing the
  2785'   D8                    	RC			;   carry: a miss still has to leave HL a
                                				;   real text address, because SCRLST puts
                                				;   the cursor there on the way to paging
  2786'   3A 2ACD'              	LDA	SSKIP
  2789'   B7                    	ORA	A
  278A'   CA 2793'              	JZ	SR1_FW
  278D'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  278E'   B1                    	ORA	C
  278F'   37                    	STC
  2790'   C8                    	RZ			; no room for a match even before the skip
  2791'   0B                    	DCX	B		; vim's '/' starts one character PAST the
  2792'   23                    	INX	H		;   cursor, so the character under it can
                                				;   never be the one that matches
  2793'                         SR1_FW:
  2793'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF
  2796'   D8                    	RC
  2797'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends PAST the match; vi lands on it
  279A'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
  279D'                         SR1_BK:
  279D'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TXTBEG
  27A0'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  27A3'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPBEG		; (as above: the pointer, then the carry)
  27A6'   D8                    	RC
  27A7'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHB already lands on the first character
  27AA'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHB
  27AD'   D8                    	RC
  27AE'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; DISPATCH TABLES -- the messages first, then CMDTAB and ACTTAB.
                                ;============================================================
                                ;============================================================
                                ; MESSAGES -- the bottom row is vim's message line: blank unless something has
                                ; been said on it.  A message is built by successive calls -- EXPRM first, which
                                ; clears the row, then the pieces straight to the terminal.  There is no
                                ; concatenation buffer and none is wanted: the pieces are a name, a flag and a
                                ; number, and each knows how to write itself.
                                ;
                                ; Every message is drawn AFTER the frame (MSGPST, from the main loop), because
                                ; a full repaint blanks the bottom row -- so a message written during the
                                ; command would be wiped by the repaint that same key causes.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;MSGNAM -- open the row and write the file name at HL (a 12-byte FCB field:
                                ; drive, 8 name, 3 type) in quotes, the way vim opens a message: "TEST.TXT".
                                ; A blank name field is vim's "[No Name]".  vim writes a path relative to the
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-122


                                ; current directory -- a file in it bare, "../x/VI.MAC" otherwise -- and the
                                ; logged drive is CP/M's current directory, so the drive is written only when
                                ; it is another one: "B:TEST.TXT".
  27B1'                         MSGNAM:
  27B1'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  27B2'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  27B4'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXPRM		; clears the row, writes the opening quote
  27B7'   E1                    	POP	H
  27B8'   E5                    	PUSH	H		; keep the base: the type field is 9 past it
  27B9'   23                    	INX	H
  27BA'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  27BB'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  27BD'   C2 27CA'              	JNZ	MN_HAS
  27C0'   21 23E4'              	LXI	H,SNONM
  27C3'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  27C6'   E1                    	POP	H
  27C7'   C3 2814'              	JMP	MN_END
  27CA'                         MN_HAS:
  27CA'   0E 19                 	MVI	C,BF_CURDSK
  27CC'   CD 0005               	CALL	BDOS		; A = the logged drive, A: = 0
  27CF'   E1                    	POP	H
  27D0'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  27D1'   3C                    	INR	A		; the FCB's drive byte is explicit, A: = 1
  27D2'   BE                    	CMP	M
  27D3'   CA 27E1'              	JZ	MN_NAM
  27D6'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  27D7'   C6 40                 	ADI	'A'-1
  27D9'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  27DC'   3E 3A                 	MVI	A,':'
  27DE'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  27E1'                         MN_NAM:
  27E1'   E1                    	POP	H
  27E2'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  27E3'   23                    	INX	H
  27E4'   0E 08                 	MVI	C,8
  27E6'                         MN_NM:
  27E6'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  27E7'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  27E9'   CA 27F4'              	JZ	MN_EXT
  27EC'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  27EF'   23                    	INX	H
  27F0'   0D                    	DCR	C
  27F1'   C2 27E6'              	JNZ	MN_NM
  27F4'                         MN_EXT:
  27F4'   E1                    	POP	H
  27F5'   11 0009               	LXI	D,9
  27F8'   19                    	DAD	D		; the type field
  27F9'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  27FA'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  27FC'   CA 2814'              	JZ	MN_END
  27FF'   3E 2E                 	MVI	A,'.'
  2801'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2804'   0E 03                 	MVI	C,3
  2806'                         MN_XL:
  2806'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2807'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-123


  2809'   CA 2814'              	JZ	MN_END
  280C'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  280F'   23                    	INX	H
  2810'   0D                    	DCR	C
  2811'   C2 2806'              	JNZ	MN_XL
  2814'                         MN_END:
  2814'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  2816'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTCH
                                
                                ;MSGFLG -- the flags after the name, in vim's order and with vim's spelling:
                                ; [Modified][readonly][New], run together with ONE space before the first of
                                ; them (measured from vim 9.1).  A = 0 leaves [Modified] out, which is the load
                                ; message, where nothing has been changed yet.
  2819'                         MSGFLG:
  2819'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; B: a flag has been written (the space goes once)
  281B'   B7                    	ORA	A
  281C'   CA 282C'              	JZ	MF_RO
  281F'   3A 2A1C'              	LDA	MODF
  2822'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2823'   CA 282C'              	JZ	MF_RO
  2826'   21 23EE'              	LXI	H,SMODM
  2829'   CD 2841'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  282C'                         MF_RO:
  282C'   3A 2A87'              	LDA	ROFLAG
  282F'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2830'   CA 2839'              	JZ	MF_NEW
  2833'   21 23F9'              	LXI	H,SROM
  2836'   CD 2841'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  2839'                         MF_NEW:
  2839'   3A 2A85'              	LDA	MSGNEW
  283C'   B7                    	ORA	A
  283D'   C8                    	RZ
  283E'   21 2404'              	LXI	H,SNEWM
  2841'                         MF_PUT:
  2841'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2842'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2843'   C2 284F'              	JNZ	MF_STR
  2846'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  2848'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2849'   3E 20                 	MVI	A,' '
  284B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  284E'   E1                    	POP	H
  284F'                         MF_STR:
  284F'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;EMITW -- HL as unsigned decimal, no leading zeros (0 still prints one digit).
                                ; Repeated subtraction of each power of ten, which is all an 8080 has.  B
                                ; carries 'a digit has been printed' so the leading zeros are skipped.
  2852'                         EMITW:
  2852'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  2853'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  2854'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  2856'   11 2710               	LXI	D,10000
  2859'   CD 2877'              	CALL	EWDIG
  285C'   11 03E8               	LXI	D,1000
  285F'   CD 2877'              	CALL	EWDIG
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-124


  2862'   11 0064               	LXI	D,100
  2865'   CD 2877'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2868'   11 000A               	LXI	D,10
  286B'   CD 2877'              	CALL	EWDIG
  286E'   7D                    	MOV	A,L		; the units digit is always printed
  286F'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  2871'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2874'   D1                    	POP	D
  2875'   C1                    	POP	B
  2876'   C9                    	RET
  2877'                         EWDIG:
  2877'   0E 00                 	MVI	C,0
  2879'                         EW_LP:
  2879'   7D                    	MOV	A,L
  287A'   93                    	SUB	E
  287B'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  287C'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  287D'   9A                    	SBB	D
  287E'   67                    	MOV	H,A
  287F'   DA 2886'              	JC	EW_DN
  2882'   0C                    	INR	C
  2883'   C3 2879'              	JMP	EW_LP
  2886'                         EW_DN:
  2886'   19                    	DAD	D		; one subtraction too many: give it back
  2887'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2888'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2889'   C2 288F'              	JNZ	EW_PR
  288C'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  288D'   B7                    	ORA	A
  288E'   C8                    	RZ			; a leading zero: write nothing
  288F'                         EW_PR:
  288F'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2890'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  2892'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2895'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  2897'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;ISEMPT -- Z iff the buffer holds nothing at all: no resident text, and nothing
                                ; spilled behind the window either.  vim's '--No lines in buffer--', shortened.
  2898'                         ISEMPT:
  2898'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	OUTPOS
  289B'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  289C'   B5                    	ORA	L
  289D'   C0                    	RNZ
  289E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	LOGLEN
  28A1'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  28A2'   B5                    	ORA	L
  28A3'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;MSGCTG -- '^G': the file, its flags, and where the cursor is.  DEVIATION: vim
                                ; also gives the file's line total and a percentage of it; no line total is kept
                                ; anywhere here, and counting one means a sweep of the whole file out to EOF and
                                ; a page back -- which would cost the undo region (PGTOPM) as well as the wait.
  28A4'                         MSGCTG:
  28A4'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  28A7'   CD 27B1'              	CALL	MSGNAM
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-125


  28AA'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1		; '^G' is where vim shows [Modified]
  28AC'   CD 2819'              	CALL	MSGFLG
  28AF'   CD 2898'              	CALL	ISEMPT
  28B2'   C2 28BB'              	JNZ	MG_POS
  28B5'   21 2417'              	LXI	H,SNOLIN
  28B8'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
  28BB'                         MG_POS:
  28BB'   21 240A'              	LXI	H,SLINE
  28BE'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  28C1'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CNTLF		; the line feeds before the cursor ...
  28C4'   23                    	INX	H		;   ... so the line it is on
  28C5'   CD 2852'              	CALL	EMITW
  28C8'   21 2411'              	LXI	H,SCOLM
  28CB'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  28CE'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CBOL		; the byte column within the line, 0-based
  28D1'   23                    	INX	H
  28D2'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  28D3'   CD 2852'              	CALL	EMITW
  28D6'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CURDCL		; the column a TAB expands that byte to
  28D9'   23                    	INX	H
  28DA'   D1                    	POP	D
  28DB'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CMP16		; the same -> vim writes one number, not two
  28DE'   C8                    	RZ
  28DF'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  28E0'   3E 2D                 	MVI	A,'-'
  28E2'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  28E5'   E1                    	POP	H
  28E6'   C3 2852'              	JMP	EMITW
                                
                                ;FILMSG -- the load message: the file and its flags.  vim adds the line and
                                ; byte totals ('3L, 30B'); the line total is the sweep MSGCTG's note describes.
                                ; A buffer with no file name gets no message at all, as in vim.
  28E9'                         FILMSG:
  28E9'   3A 0001*              	LDA	FNAME+1
  28EC'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  28EE'   C8                    	RZ
  28EF'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  28F2'   CD 27B1'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  28F5'   AF                    	XRA	A		; nothing has been changed yet
  28F6'   C3 2819'              	JMP	MSGFLG
                                
                                ;WRMSG -- what ':w' says.  vim counts what it wrote ('"TEST.TXT" 3L, 29B
                                ; written'); the counts are the same sweep, so this says only that it happened.
                                ; The name is TFCB's, so ':w OTHER.TXT' names the file it really wrote.
  28F9'                         WRMSG:
  28F9'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TFCB
  28FC'   CD 27B1'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  28FF'   21 2429'              	LXI	H,SWRIT
  2902'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;MSGPST -- draw the message this key asked for, once the frame is painted, and
                                ; put the cursor back.  The main loop calls it after SCDRAW and MODEMS.
  2905'                         MSGPST:
  2905'   3A 2A84'              	LDA	MSGWHT
  2908'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2909'   C8                    	RZ
